Condition monitoring, diagnostics and failure hypotheses (@pe-diagnostics: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @electrical-power-engineering, #source "../sources/Transformer-Maintenance-and-Diagnostics-FIST.lingua#r_5Y3NB9GQFCVNJ8QVR9BC53JS32", #source "../sources/Maintenance-of-Power-Circuit-Breakers-FIST.lingua#r_6YDRQ4GKWPW34WTZ4AVJZHGKP4", #source "../sources/Hydroelectric-Mechanical-and-Electrical-Design-USACE.lingua#r_1N0D32HTSXMVJ1VRW9TNTYV9P8", #source "../sources/Motor-Rewind-Study-and-Good-Practice-EASA-AEMT.lingua#r_3K38XSH8DWC79G20NF55ATKEJP", #electrical-power-engineering "Maintenance-outages-spares-repair-and-return-to-service.lingua#r_7DVW7TWDKKPHWD780FYGQDCHFB") { r_6P53MN2MTNTE5PQ585XX6YFD0E
**Read:** [Transformer maintenance and diagnostics — FIST 3-30 and 3-31](../sources/Transformer-Maintenance-and-Diagnostics-FIST.lingua#r_5Y3NB9GQFCVNJ8QVR9BC53JS32), both entire manuals and applicable complete diagnostic methods; [Maintenance of Power Circuit Breakers — FIST 3-16](../sources/Maintenance-of-Power-Circuit-Breakers-FIST.lingua#r_6YDRQ4GKWPW34WTZ4AVJZHGKP4), complete condition/test sections; [Mechanical and Electrical Design of Hydroelectric Power Plants — USACE](../sources/Hydroelectric-Mechanical-and-Electrical-Design-USACE.lingua#r_1N0D32HTSXMVJ1VRW9TNTYV9P8), whole machine-condition-monitoring chapter; [Motor rewind studies and good practice — EASA/AEMT](../sources/Motor-Rewind-Study-and-Good-Practice-EASA-AEMT.lingua#r_3K38XSH8DWC79G20NF55ATKEJP), full methods, loss/error and repair evidence.

A condition indicator is a measurement interpreted through a failure/material model. Temperature, vibration, insulation response, gas/oil analysis, partial-discharge evidence, contact resistance and timing probe different mechanisms. A single abnormal value is not automatically a unique diagnosis or an immediate remaining-life estimate.

For a documented transformer, machine or breaker, state the failure hypotheses, observed quantities, calibration/uncertainty, baseline and operating history. Compare an alternative cause and a second independent observable. A change in load, temperature, sensor or test method can change the reading without the inferred damage mechanism.

Read complete applicable [Transformer maintenance and diagnostics — FIST 3-30 and 3-31](../sources/Transformer-Maintenance-and-Diagnostics-FIST.lingua#r_5Y3NB9GQFCVNJ8QVR9BC53JS32), [Maintenance of Power Circuit Breakers — FIST 3-16](../sources/Maintenance-of-Power-Circuit-Breakers-FIST.lingua#r_6YDRQ4GKWPW34WTZ4AVJZHGKP4), [Motor rewind studies and good practice — EASA/AEMT](../sources/Motor-Rewind-Study-and-Good-Practice-EASA-AEMT.lingua#r_3K38XSH8DWC79G20NF55ATKEJP) and [Mechanical and Electrical Design of Hydroelectric Power Plants — USACE](../sources/Hydroelectric-Mechanical-and-Electrical-Design-USACE.lingua#r_1N0D32HTSXMVJ1VRW9TNTYV9P8) methods. Trace the result to a technically justified service, inspection, repair or replacement decision with actual limits and consequence. Distinguish correlation from a causal failure explanation and a forecast from a measured state. Preserve negative/inconclusive evidence and the next test that would change the decision. This is a practical scholarly investigation of a maintained physical system, not a promise that more sensors alone produce reliable diagnosis.

